This notice carries two facts about what this software does and does not
guarantee. Apache-2.0 §4(d) requires them to travel with any redistribution,
which is why they are here rather than only in the documentation.

1. THE CREDENTIAL THIS SOFTWARE HOLDS CAN TRADE.

   Robinhood advertises a single OAuth scope, `internal`. There is no
   read-only scope to request. A token obtained by this software is capable of
   placing, cancelling, and exercising orders, whatever this software chooses
   to do with it.

   What restrains it is a human-reviewed manifest committed to this package,
   plus exact digest comparisons made before every call. That boundary is
   "no trading", not "no writes": the reviewed manifest denies all six order
   tools and both order simulators, and allows a set of non-trading mutations
   (watchlist and saved-scan management) alongside its reads. Each capability
   carries a reviewed `mutates` flag.

   A fork that edits the manifest, weakens the digest checks, or ships its own
   manifest changes this property. Apache-2.0 §4(b) requires such a fork to
   carry prominent notice of the modification. For this software that
   requirement is not a formality — the manifest is the entire security
   boundary, and a consumer pinning a digest is relying on it.

2. THIS SOFTWARE HAS NOT HAD AN INDEPENDENT SECURITY REVIEW.

   Its own design document (DESIGN.md §12) lists one as a release-acceptance
   requirement. That requirement has been deliberately waived, not met.

   Every review performed to date was conducted by agents operating under the
   same orchestration as the implementation. Those reviews found real defects
   — a fail-open argument-validation bypass, a callback-abort bypass, a
   credential record reaching a public repr, an account identifier reaching
   consumer-visible JSON — and each was fixed and covered by tests. But they
   were not independent in the sense §12 means: no party outside the
   development of this software has examined it.

   Anyone deploying this against a real brokerage account should read that as
   what it is.

